- <main id="start"><section class="hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">Workshop · Nature · Technology</p><h1>Preserving the old.<br>Discovering the new.</h1><p class="lead">Welcome to Oppi's Hobbies: stories from the motorcycle workshop, small wonders from the macro world, and practical projects around Linux and computers.</p><a class="button" href="#projects">View my projects</a></div><div class="stamp"><img src="../images/sticker.png" alt="Oppi's Hobbies: motorcycle, nature and technology"></div></section>
--<section class="section workshop-update"><p class="eyebrow">Workshop update</p><h2>New from the workshop</h2><div class="workshop-grid"><div><h3>Honda CB250G · Rebuild under way</h3><p>The frame has been powder-coated, and the freshly rebuilt engine is back in place: new pistons, a rebored and honed cylinder, lapped valves, a new timing chain and adjusted valve clearances.</p><p>Now the careful reassembly begins. Missing parts still need to be found; the tank and side covers are waiting for <strong>Hawaiian Blue Metallic</strong>. The motorcycle came from Denmark but has Swiss registration papers, so customs clearance is also part of the project.</p><ol class="milestones"><li class="done">Frame derusted &amp; powder-coated</li><li class="done">Engine rebuilt &amp; installed</li><li class="current">Fittings &amp; missing parts</li><li>Paint, customs &amp; final assembly</li></ol><p class="next-step"><b>Next step:</b> Sort the fittings, find the missing parts and prepare the next stages of assembly.</p><a class="button" href="projekte/cb250g/index.html">View project</a></div><img src="../images/cb250g/zuendung-eingestellt.jpg" alt="Set ignition timing on the Honda CB250G"></div><article class="workshop-note"><img src="../images/cb250g/ventilfederspanner-eigenbau.jpg" alt="Homemade valve spring tool for the Honda CB250G"><div><p class="eyebrow">Small workshop idea</p><h3>Fitting valves – without needing three hands</h3><p>Removing valves is often manageable. Reinstalling them becomes fiddly: the spring must be compressed, the valve must not slip down – and the two tiny valve collets have to be refitted at the top of the valve stem.</p><p>So I made this small holding tool for the CB250G. Clamped in a bench clamp, it compresses the valve spring in a controlled way while holding the valve in position. That leaves both hands free to remove or fit the collets carefully. Whether it will fit other Honda models would need to be checked individually; I would be happy to lend it if useful.</p></div></article><article class="workshop-note"><img src="../images/cb250g/auspuffflansch-klemmhuelsen.jpg" alt="New stainless-steel exhaust clamp sleeves beside rusted original parts"><div><p class="eyebrow">Care for detail</p><h3>New clamp sleeves for the exhaust headers</h3><p>The exhaust headers are also secured to the cylinder head with split clamp sleeves and exhaust flanges. The original parts were badly rusted.</p><p>On the left is a new pair of stainless-steel clamp sleeves, made on the lathe; on the right are the old original parts for comparison. These small solutions will hardly be seen later – but that attention to detail is one of the pleasures of a rebuild.</p></div></article></section>
-+<section class="section workshop-update"><p class="eyebrow">Workshop update</p><h2>New from the workshop</h2><div class="workshop-grid"><div><h3>Honda CB250G · Rebuild under way</h3><p>The frame has been powder-coated, and the freshly rebuilt engine is back in place: new pistons, a rebored and honed cylinder, lapped valves, a new timing chain and adjusted valve clearances.</p><p>Now the careful reassembly begins. Missing parts still need to be found; the tank and side covers are waiting for <strong>Hawaiian Blue Metallic</strong>. The motorcycle came from Denmark but has Swiss registration papers, so customs clearance is also part of the project.</p><ol class="milestones"><li class="done">Frame derusted &amp; powder-coated</li><li class="done">Engine rebuilt &amp; installed</li><li class="current">Fittings &amp; missing parts</li><li>Paint, customs &amp; final assembly</li></ol><p class="next-step"><b>Next step:</b> Sort the fittings, find the missing parts and prepare the next stages of assembly.</p><a class="button" href="projekte/cb250g/index.html">View project</a></div><img src="../images/cb250g/zuendung-eingestellt.jpg" alt="Set ignition timing on the Honda CB250G"></div><article class="workshop-note"><img src="../images/cb250g/kolben-desaster.jpg" alt="Severely damaged piston from a Honda CB250 engine"><div><p class="eyebrow">A look inside the engine</p><h3>Piston disaster</h3><p>Thankfully, this is not my CB250G but another project belonging to my brother-in-law. We got the engine running again, but it was breathing heavily through the crankcase vent and the left cylinder had hardly any power.</p><p>The warning signs were clear. The engine was out in about an hour, and soon afterwards the cause was on the workbench: the left piston and its ring grooves were severely damaged. A good reminder that, with an old restoration project, it pays to look inside the engine before the first longer run.</p><p>Fortunately, the repair is straightforward: a suitable cylinder and piston are already available. Only new piston rings and a gasket set still need to be ordered – then the engine should soon be running again.</p></div></article><article class="workshop-note"><img src="../images/cb250g/ventilfederspanner-eigenbau.jpg" alt="Homemade valve spring tool for the Honda CB250G"><div><p class="eyebrow">Small workshop idea</p><h3>Fitting valves – without needing three hands</h3><p>Removing valves is often manageable. Reinstalling them becomes fiddly: the spring must be compressed, the valve must not slip down – and the two tiny valve collets have to be refitted at the top of the valve stem.</p><p>So I made this small holding tool for the CB250G. Clamped in a bench clamp, it compresses the valve spring in a controlled way while holding the valve in position. That leaves both hands free to remove or fit the collets carefully. Whether it will fit other Honda models would need to be checked individually; I would be happy to lend it if useful.</p></div></article><article class="workshop-note"><img src="../images/cb250g/auspuffflansch-klemmhuelsen.jpg" alt="New stainless-steel exhaust clamp sleeves beside rusted original parts"><div><p class="eyebrow">Care for detail</p><h3>New clamp sleeves for the exhaust headers</h3><p>The exhaust headers are also secured to the cylinder head with split clamp sleeves and exhaust flanges. The original parts were badly rusted.</p><p>On the left is a new pair of stainless-steel clamp sleeves, made on the lathe; on the right are the old original parts for comparison. These small solutions will hardly be seen later – but that attention to detail is one of the pleasures of a rebuild.</p></div></article></section>
