 <section id="cb900f" class="section"><p class="eyebrow">06 · Completed project</p><h2>Honda CB900F Bol d'Or restored</h2><div class="project-layout"><div class="year">1981</div><div><h3>Honda CB900F Bol d'Or</h3><p>From the failed engine to tyres and final drive: the big Bol d'Or was restored from the ground up – and later exchanged for a lighter 1970 CB450.</p><a class="button" href="projekte/cb900f/index.html">Story and gallery</a></div><img class="project-illustration" src="../images/cb900f/ausgangszustand.jpg" alt="Honda CB900F Bol d'Or before restoration"></div></section>
 <section id="cb450" class="section"><p class="eyebrow">07 · Completed project</p><h2>Honda CB450K1 Black Bomber restored</h2><div class="project-layout"><div class="year">1970</div><div><h3>Honda CB450K1 Black Bomber</h3><p>After being exchanged for the CB900F, the lighter CB450 received a more thorough restoration than planned – and is now the more interesting motorcycle to me.</p><a class="button" href="projekte/cb450/index.html">Story and gallery</a></div><img class="project-illustration" src="../images/cb450/ausgangszustand.jpg" alt="Honda CB450K1 Black Bomber when acquired"></div></section>
 <section class="section site-story"><p class="eyebrow">08 · The idea behind it</p><h2>Preserving the old. Looking closely.</h2><div class="story-copy"><p>This website brings together things that have stayed with me for many years: classic motorcycles, their technology and the small wonders of nature.</p><p>From 1998 to 2010, I lived near Durban in South Africa. That is where my macro photography began: often, stepping out of the living room was enough to be surrounded by an astonishingly varied world of insects. Until 2014, I tried to make the small things large – animals and details that are otherwise easily overlooked. The most beautiful photographs were often taken very early in the morning, when dew still lay on the grass and the insects were cool and unhurried. Germany also has plenty to discover when one takes the time to look closely.</p><p>My motorcycles date roughly from 1950 to 1990 – from my own time, not from a distant past. I ride and restore them because their technology is still understandable, and because many parts can be repaired or even made anew on the lathe. Work in the workshop is a special balance for me: time disappears, as do many of the things that otherwise occupy my mind. And riding is not about speed. When the engine is running smoothly and sounding right at around 80 km/h, that is already a very good day for me.</p></div></section>
